<releaseinfo>0.1.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&tdemixer; is a volume mixer for &pipewire; and &pulseaudio;. It sets the
volume of every application that plays or records sound and of every sound
device, and replaces &kmix; in the system tray. It is available as a
standalone application, as a Trinity Control Center module, in System
Settings and as a system tray icon.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>Trinity</keyword>
<keyword>volume</keyword>
<keyword>mixer</keyword>
<keyword>PipeWire</keyword>
<keyword>PulseAudio</keyword>
<keyword>KMix</keyword>
<keyword>sound</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
Modern Linux systems play sound through a <emphasis>sound server</emphasis>,
&pipewire; or &pulseaudio;, instead of opening the sound card directly.
The sound server mixes the sound of all applications, so each application
has its own volume, and it can send each application to a different
device: music to the speakers and a video call to a headset, for example.
</para>

<para>
&kmix;, the mixer of older TDE releases, controls the channels of the sound
card itself (Master, PCM, Line, ...). &tdemixer; controls the sound server
instead. It shows:
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>every application that is playing or recording sound, with
its own volume, mute button, level meter and the device it uses;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>every output device (speakers, headphones, HDMI, Bluetooth,
...) and input device (microphones), with volume, mute, the port (for
example speakers or headphones jack) and which device is the default;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>every sound card with its profile (analog stereo, surround,
HDMI, headset modes, off).</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
It talks to &pipewire; directly or uses the &pulseaudio; protocol, which
works with a &pulseaudio; daemon and with &pipewire;'s pulse server
(<command>pipewire-pulse</command>). See <xref linkend="backends"/>.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Playback page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="playback.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Playback page with three applications</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="getting-started">
<title>Getting Started</title>

<sect1 id="requirements">
<title>Requirements</title>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>&pipewire; with WirePlumber and <command>pipewire-pulse</command>
(the default on Debian 12 and 13 desktops), or a &pulseaudio; daemon.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The Trinity Desktop Environment R14.1 or later.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="starting">
<title>Starting the Mixer</title>
<para>&tdemixer; can be used in four ways; all show the same mixer.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>System tray</term>
<listitem><para>
The volume icon in the panel's system tray starts automatically at login.
See <xref linkend="tray"/>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term>Standalone application</term>
<listitem><para>
Choose <guimenuitem>Volume Mixer</guimenuitem> in the
<guimenu>Multimedia</guimenu> section of the TDE menu, choose
<guimenuitem>Open Mixer</guimenuitem> in the tray icon's menu, or run
<userinput><command>tde-mixer</command></userinput>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term>Trinity Control Center</term>
<listitem><para>
Open the Control Center and choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Sound &amp; Multimedia</guimenu><guimenuitem>Volume Mixer</guimenuitem></menuchoice>,
or run <userinput><command>tdecmshell</command> <option>mixer</option></userinput>.
The module has an additional <guilabel>Settings</guilabel> tab, see
<xref linkend="settings"/>.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Volume Mixer in the Control Center</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="kcontrol.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Volume Mixer module in the Trinity Control Center</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term>System Settings</term>
<listitem><para>
In System Settings (<command>systemsettings</command>), the module is in
<guilabel>Computer Administration</guilabel>, next to
<guilabel>Sound System</guilabel>.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>System Settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="systemsettings.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Volume Mixer in System Settings</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="using">
<title>Using the Mixer</title>

<para>
The mixer has five pages. Every change takes effect at once and is
remembered by the sound server; there is nothing to save.
</para>

<sect1 id="rows">
<title>Volume Rows</title>
<para>
Every application and device is shown as a row with:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Icon and name</term>
<listitem><para>For an application, its name and what it is playing (the
<quote>media name</quote>, for example the title of a song or video);
<quote>(paused)</quote> is added while it is paused. For a device, its
description and its internal name.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Volume slider</term>
<listitem><para>From 0 to 100 %, or to 150 % if amplification is allowed in
the settings. The slider keeps the balance between the channels: if the
left channel is quieter than the right one, both are moved together. The
tooltip of the slider shows the volume of each channel.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Mute button</term>
<listitem><para>The speaker button left of the slider mutes and unmutes.
Its icon shows the volume.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Level meter</term>
<listitem><para>The bar under the slider shows how loud the sound is right
now: green, then yellow above 75 % and red above 95 % of full scale. The
meters can be turned off in the settings.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="playback">
<title>Playback</title>
<para>
One row for each application that is playing sound. The list at the top
right of a row selects the output device: choose another device to move
the application there, for example from the speakers to headphones. The
application keeps playing.
</para>
<para>
The sound server remembers the volume and the device of each application,
and uses them the next time the application plays sound.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="recording">
<title>Recording</title>
<para>
One row for each application that records sound, with the input device it
records from. Besides microphones, the list offers the
<quote>monitor</quote> of each output device, which records what is played
on it.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Recording page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="recording.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>An application recording from the microphone</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="output-devices">
<title>Output Devices</title>
<para>
The speakers, headphones, HDMI outputs and other output devices.
<guilabel>Default</guilabel> marks the device that new sounds are played
on; select it on another device to make that the default. Applications that
were not moved to a device by hand follow the default.
</para>
<para>
If a device has more than one <guilabel>Port</guilabel>, for example
<quote>Speakers</quote> and <quote>Headphones</quote> on a laptop's sound
card, the port can be chosen from the list. Unplugged ports are marked.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Output Devices page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="output-devices.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Two output devices; Speakers is the default</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="input-devices">
<title>Input Devices</title>
<para>
The microphones and other inputs, with the same controls as the output
devices. Monitor sources are not listed here.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="configuration">
<title>Configuration</title>
<para>
One row for each sound card with its <guilabel>Profile</guilabel>. The
profile decides which devices a card offers: for example <quote>Analog
Stereo Duplex</quote> (speakers and microphone), <quote>Digital Stereo
(HDMI)</quote>, a surround setup, or <quote>Off</quote>. Bluetooth headsets
offer a high quality playback profile and a headset profile with a
microphone. Profiles that cannot be used right now (for example HDMI without
a monitor connected) are marked <quote>unavailable</quote>.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="tray">
<title>The System Tray Icon</title>

<para>
<command>tde-mixer-tray</command> shows a speaker in the panel's system
tray. It replaces &kmix;'s tray icon and starts automatically at login.
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Icon and tooltip</term>
<listitem><para>The icon shows the volume of the default output device, or
that it is muted. The tooltip shows the volume, the device and how many
applications are playing sound. The icon is gray while no sound server is
reachable.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Left click</term>
<listitem><para>Opens a small window with the volume of the default output
device and of each application that plays sound. <guibutton>Mixer...</guibutton>
opens the full mixer.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Middle click</term>
<listitem><para>Mutes or unmutes the default output device.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Mouse wheel</term>
<listitem><para>Changes the volume of the default output device by the
volume step (5 % unless changed in the settings).</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Right click</term>
<listitem><para>The menu offers <guimenuitem>Open Mixer</guimenuitem>, the
<guisubmenu>Output Device</guisubmenu> to make another device the default,
<guimenuitem>Mute</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Configure Volume Control...</guimenuitem>
(the Control Center module), <guimenuitem>Configure Shortcuts...</guimenuitem>
and <guimenuitem>Start Automatically at Login</guimenuitem>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The tray icon's window</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="tray-popup.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The volume window of the tray icon</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<sect1 id="volume-keys">
<title>Volume Keys</title>
<para>
While the tray icon runs, the volume keys of the keyboard work:
<keycap>Volume Up</keycap> and <keycap>Volume Down</keycap> change the
volume of the default output device by the volume step,
<keycap>Mute</keycap> mutes it and <keycap>Microphone Mute</keycap> mutes
the default input device. Other keys can be chosen with
<guimenuitem>Configure Shortcuts...</guimenuitem> in the tray icon's menu.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="replacing-kmix">
<title>Replacing KMix</title>
<para>
&tdemixer; can be installed next to &kmix;. If &kmix; is set to start at
login, the tray icon asks once, when it starts for the first time, whether
&kmix; should be turned off. Choosing <guibutton>Turn KMix Off</guibutton>
sets <literal>Autostart=false</literal> in &kmix;'s configuration
(<filename>kmixrc</filename>) and quits a running &kmix;. &kmix; stays
installed and can be started from the menu at any time; to start it at
login again, enable <guilabel>Restore on login</guilabel> in &kmix;'s settings.
</para>
<para>
The question can be turned off with <guilabel>Offer to turn off KMix's tray
icon</guilabel> in the settings.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="settings">
<title>Settings</title>

<para>
The settings are on the <guilabel>Settings</guilabel> tab of the Control
Center module, and in <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure
Volume Mixer...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> of the standalone mixer.
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Backend</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How the mixer talks to the sound server, see
<xref linkend="backends"/>. A change takes effect the next time the mixer,
the module or the tray icon starts.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Step for the volume keys and the mouse wheel</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>From 1 to 25 %, 5 % by default.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Allow volumes above 100 %</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Lets the sliders go up to 150 %. Sound above 100 % is
amplified in software and can be distorted.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Show level meters</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the level meters under the sliders. The meters
record a small amount of sound from each device and application, which
costs a little processor time.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Start the volume control in the system tray at login</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Same as <guimenuitem>Start Automatically at Login</guimenuitem>
in the tray icon's menu.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Offer to turn off KMix's tray icon</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>See <xref linkend="replacing-kmix"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Settings tab</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Settings tab of the Control Center module</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="backends">
<title>PipeWire and PulseAudio</title>

<para>
&tdemixer; has two backends:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>PipeWire</term>
<listitem><para>
Talks to &pipewire; directly. Volumes of sound cards are set on the card's
active route, so that WirePlumber saves them, and applications are moved
and defaults chosen through WirePlumber's settings
(<quote>metadata</quote>), the same way <command>wpctl</command> and
<command>pipewire-pulse</command> do it.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>PulseAudio</term>
<listitem><para>
Uses the &pulseaudio; protocol (<filename>libpulse</filename>). This works
with a &pulseaudio; daemon and with &pipewire;'s pulse server, so it is also
a fallback on &pipewire; systems.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
<guilabel>Automatic</guilabel>, the default, uses the &pipewire; backend if
a &pipewire; daemon is running and the &pulseaudio; backend otherwise. The
backend in use is shown at the bottom of the mixer. If the sound server is
restarted, the mixer reconnects by itself.
</para>

<para>
Both backends show the same thing. Applications using ALSA directly
without going through the sound server (rare on current systems) are not
listed.
</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command Line</title>

<para>
<command>tde-mixer</command> accepts these options besides the generic TDE
and TQt options:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><option>--page</option> <replaceable>page</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>The page to show: <literal>playback</literal>,
<literal>recording</literal>, <literal>output</literal>, <literal>input</literal>
or <literal>configuration</literal>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><option>--backend</option> <replaceable>backend</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para><literal>auto</literal>, <literal>pipewire</literal> or
<literal>pulse</literal>, instead of the backend in the settings.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><option>--list</option></term>
<listitem><para>Prints the devices, applications and sound cards and exits.
Needs no display.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><option>--volume</option> <replaceable>percent</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Sets the volume of the default output device and exits.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><option>--toggle-mute</option></term>
<listitem><para>Mutes or unmutes the default output device and exits.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
The manual pages <command>man tde-mixer</command> and <command>man
tde-mixer-tray</command> describe the programs and their files.
</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="troubleshooting">
<title>Troubleshooting</title>

<qandaset>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The mixer says <quote>Not connected to a sound server</quote>.</para></question>
<answer><para>
No sound server is running for your user. On &pipewire; systems check
<userinput><command>systemctl --user status pipewire pipewire-pulse wireplumber</command></userinput>;
on &pulseaudio; systems <userinput><command>pulseaudio --check</command></userinput>.
The mixer connects as soon as the server is up.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>An application is missing from the Playback page.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Only applications that currently have a sound stream open are shown. Many
applications only open one while they play.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>There are two volume icons in the system tray.</para></question>
<answer><para>
&kmix; is running too. Quit it and turn off its <guilabel>Restore on
login</guilabel>, or see <xref linkend="replacing-kmix"/>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>The volume keys do nothing.</para></question>
<answer><para>
The keys are handled by the tray icon, which must be running. Another
program (for example &kmix;) may have taken the keys first; quit it and
restart the tray icon. <guimenuitem>Configure Shortcuts...</guimenuitem> in the
tray icon's menu shows which keys are used.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>Moving an application to another device does nothing on
&pipewire;.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Moving needs a session manager. Check that WirePlumber is running.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

